Album sharing:
  - Support other users adding their own photos (per-user ingest tokens that set albums.owner_email)
  - Allow the same album name under different owners (photos.owner_email and an (owner_email, name) album key)
  - Per-owner people management
  - Stranger load: with Access open to any Google account, a signed-in account with no shares still runs scoped queries that scan the albums table (about one scan per statement, several per search); add a rate limit rule on /api/* and/or an album_shares(email, album) lookup table so a stranger costs about 0 rows
  - Scoped search: viewers (and the default owner once another owner exists) lose the date_time and last_indexed early exit and read and sort every visible row per page; for the unfiltered total use SUM(photo_count) over the visible albums instead of COUNT(*) over photos
  - Access hardening in functions/_lib/access.ts: negative-cache failed JWKS fetches (a forged kid can trigger a certs fetch per request while the endpoint fails), check the iss claim, serve a stale JWKS for a short grace period after the certs endpoint fails, add a timeout to the originals access subrequest
  - Vite dev proxy: fail loudly when neither DEV_VIEWER_EMAIL nor OWNER_EMAIL is set (it currently forwards a client X-Viewer-Email)
  - A removed share can still show thumbnails and originals already cached in the viewer's browser (originals 1h, thumbnails immutable); decide whether to shorten or revalidate caches for non-owners
  - Share dialog: enforce the 50 email cap in the UI (maxTags), dedupe case-insensitively, test the 400/403/404 error display, keep an email typed but not yet committed when Save is clicked, and verify the dialog and AlbumCard layout in a browser (jsdom only so far)
  - Viewer daily pick is the same photo every day until ratings change (no write path to rotate it); pick by day hash instead
  - Empty state: a signed-in user with nothing shared sees empty lists and "No photos available" on /daily; show a "Nothing has been shared with you yet" message
  - Route-enumeration test (worker/test/sharing-writes.test.ts) ignores app.all writes and does not prove an albumWrite handler really calls albumRole; wrap the check so it cannot be forgotten
  - canEditFor for the default owner still scans albums once another owner exists (the partial index cannot serve the NULL branch); revisit with multi-owner ingest
  - A photo in an album owned by someone else but shared with the default owner has path null for the agent originals lookup (R6), so its original cannot be served; handle with multi-owner ingest
  - Measure after rollout: wrangler tail pixdex-worker --format json and wrangler d1 insights pixdex --timePeriod 30d; replace the EXPLAIN-based estimates in docs/development.md with real rows read
  - Test gaps: scoped search with the people filter and with sort, timeline year zoom and undated groups for non-owners, tiered search fallback as a non-owner

API:
  - Fold every Worker route under /api/* (/api/search, /api/ingest/rating, ...) so the Pages proxy forwards paths unchanged and the agent's CLOUDFLARE_API_BASE_URL gets /api appended. Keep /api/ingest/* out of the proxy allowlist. Deploy the worker and agent together; update tests, README and functions/api/[[path]].ts

Hygience:
  - Upgrade to latest version

Performance, Worker CPU (context: the free plan allows 10 ms CPU per invocation; over the 30 days to 2026-11-09, pixdex-worker p50 was 1.7 ms, p99 27 ms, and 242 requests ended as exceededResources, so Workers Paid is currently masking this):
  - Measure first: run `wrangler tail pixdex-worker --format json` during an indexing batch, a person match and a groups load, and record cpuTime per route. Static analysis only; no route is confirmed yet. Re-check with the GraphQL workersInvocationsAdaptive quantiles (cpuTimeP99) after each fix

Performance, D1 (deferred to the December 2026 review: re-run `wrangler d1 insights pixdex --timePeriod 30d` first and only act on queries still in the top 5; from `wrangler d1 insights pixdex --timePeriod 30d`: about 21.5M rows read, 70K written, measured before the albums table and counters shipped; re-run it to see what is left):
  - Photos list `SELECT photos.* ... ORDER BY last_indexed DESC LIMIT ? OFFSET ?` (562 runs; idx_photos_last_indexed now serves it, so deep pages still cost offset + limit rows)
    - Switch from OFFSET (the UI uses page numbers and offsets, so keyset changes the API and the pager) to keyset pagination (cursor = last_indexed + id); update the frontend cursor handling and tests
  - People cover/ROW_NUMBER() PARTITION BY f.person_id query (195 runs, about 5.4K rows): store the chosen cover face on persons (persons.cover_face_id exists) and update it when faces are confirmed or unassigned or the cover changes
  - Filtered searches (q, album, dates, people) still run COUNT(*) on every request; cache or skip it on non-first pages if it shows up in the top 5
  - Re-check after the above: face matching scan `faces WHERE det_score >= 0.7 ... rowid > ?` (260 runs, about 3.6K rows, efficiency 0.05) and `faces WHERE id IN (json_each(?))` (1,509 runs, about 1K rows). Both look acceptable (paged by rowid / primary-key lookups); only act if they stay in the top 5
  - Add a note to docs/development.md on how to re-run `wrangler d1 insights` and the CPU GraphQL query, and re-run both after each change to confirm the drop
